DM: the

00:20:32.740 --> 00:20:37.660
DM: supposed fey history of Tyrwingha, but which are true and which are

00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:40.780
Unknown: mythical is sometimes hard to know.

00:20:43.940 --> 00:20:46.140
DM: And as the sun rises, you are

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:49.540
DM: in the middle of the Western Gulf.

00:20:49.540 --> 00:20:51.560
DM: The sun rises on December 14th.

00:20:52.920 --> 00:20:53.000
DM: Um,

00:20:54.560 --> 00:21:05.320
DM: uh, sorry, as the sun rises on Dec- on December 14th, you can see the Western Gulf in the, in the distance, the shimmering sea that Wellby spent most of his childhood

00:21:05.320 --> 00:21:06.140
DM: sailing around.

00:21:06.920 --> 00:21:07.300
Seeker: Hmm.

00:21:09.460 --> 00:21:09.680
DM: Um,

00:21:11.340 --> 00:21:11.380
DM: now-

00:21:11.380 --> 00:21:13.420
Kenzo: Wellby, fond memories of this place, Wellby?

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:14.600
Delwath: What was that like?

00:21:17.540 --> 00:21:19.180
Wellby: It was beautiful and amazing and

00:21:20.540 --> 00:21:22.140
Wellby: deeply boring after a while.

00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:27.660
Wellby: I don't think I set foot on land f- till I was, like, four or five.

00:21:28.580 --> 00:21:35.100
Wellby: It was just all on boats, and it was incredible until you go back to the same port for the 15th time.

00:21:36.160 --> 00:21:39.780
Wellby: It's all... And Sembara is less exciting than it seems.

00:21:39.800 --> 00:21:45.920
Wellby: It's all... It's castles and fields, and then it's castles and fields, and then it's fields and fields and castles.

00:21:45.920 --> 00:21:46.000
Wellby: It-

00:21:47.300 --> 00:21:48.540
Seeker: The gulf looks pretty big from here.

00:21:48.540 --> 00:21:50.120
Seeker: How, how long to get all the way around?

00:21:50.900 --> 00:21:52.320
Seeker: Or would you kind of do a circuit?

00:21:53.660 --> 00:22:00.640
Wellby: We would. We'd go back and forth. So we'd go from Tollen and then put in, sail up the rivers to the cities, and then...

00:22:01.680 --> 00:22:07.180
Wellby: God, I must have been 12 or 13 my parents finally decided they were done on the ocean

00:22:08.440 --> 00:22:12.900
Wellby: and set up a wagon caravan, and we'd just do a circuit through Sembara

00:22:14.120 --> 00:22:15.860
Wellby: from one town to the next.

00:22:15.860 --> 00:22:16.480
Wellby: And that-

00:22:16.500 --> 00:22:17.160
Kenzo: Overland? Mm.

00:22:17.280 --> 00:22:18.080
Wellby: Overland, yeah.

00:22:19.580 --> 00:22:21.700
Wellby: Is eventually what drove me out of Sembara,

00:22:23.160 --> 00:22:27.100
Wellby: because it's castles and it's fields, and then it's a differently shaped castle-

00:22:27.240 --> 00:22:27.500
Seeker: Hmm

00:22:27.500 --> 00:22:31.880
Wellby: ... and a differently shaped field, and it's, it's the same thing over and over and over again.

00:22:31.880 --> 00:22:32.020
Wellby: But-

00:22:32.420 --> 00:22:33.040
Seeker: Sounds nice.

00:22:34.520 --> 00:22:34.760
Wellby: Mm.

00:22:34.760 --> 00:22:35.200
Delwath: Peaceful.

00:22:36.880 --> 00:22:37.580
Wellby: Dull.

00:22:37.580 --> 00:22:39.140
Seeker: Some might enjoy that kind of routine.

00:22:39.820 --> 00:22:41.260
Wellby: I'm sure the people who live there...

00:22:41.260 --> 00:22:47.260
Wellby: I mean, they have a much better quality of life than, you know, living in eastern Dunmar and being attacked by

00:22:48.300 --> 00:22:49.580
Wellby: beasts and orcs and-

00:22:49.600 --> 00:22:52.680
Kenzo: Being a street orphan in Chardine getting burned by jaylight.

00:22:53.920 --> 00:22:54.920
Wellby: Yeah, I mean, it's better than that.

00:22:54.920 --> 00:22:58.380
Unknown: [laughs]

00:22:58.580 --> 00:22:59.620
Wellby: That's for sure. But I mean, like-

00:22:59.660 --> 00:23:01.320
Delwath: Sitting in an extra dimensional world.

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:02.800
Wellby: [laughs]

00:23:02.800 --> 00:23:02.960
DM: [laughs]

00:23:02.960 --> 00:23:03.220
Unknown: [laughs]

00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:05.600
Unknown: I'm sure people in Sembara...

00:23:07.560 --> 00:23:07.760
DM: [laughs]

00:23:07.760 --> 00:23:08.380
Wellby: I'm sure...

00:23:09.220 --> 00:23:09.900
Wellby: It, it was an extra-

00:23:09.900 --> 00:23:10.400
Unknown: [laughs]

00:23:10.420 --> 00:23:14.360
Wellby: I'm sure people in Sembara had all of those things, but as a halfling in Sembara,

00:23:17.000 --> 00:23:18.440
Wellby: it, it was the routine of it.

00:23:19.120 --> 00:23:20.480
Wellby: And the gulf was beautiful.

00:23:20.480 --> 00:23:20.860
Unknown: [laughs]

00:23:20.860 --> 00:23:23.340
Wellby: And the water was never the same, but once we were off the water,

00:23:24.980 --> 00:23:25.280
Wellby: it was-

00:23:25.440 --> 00:23:29.040
Kenzo: Somebody's making a Talgar TikTok right now about halfling privilege and-

00:23:29.040 --> 00:23:34.120
Wellby: [laughs]

00:23:34.120 --> 00:23:34.280
Unknown: [laughs]

00:23:34.320 --> 00:23:34.340
DM: Yeah.

00:23:34.360 --> 00:23:35.640
Wellby: At least you had a home, though.

00:23:35.640 --> 00:23:36.660
Unknown: [laughs]

00:23:36.680 --> 00:23:37.880
Wellby: You have a place you're from.

00:23:39.380 --> 00:23:41.000
Kenzo: You're, you don't, but you don't have a place you're from?

00:23:41.740 --> 00:23:42.620
Wellby: No, halflings don't.

00:23:44.840 --> 00:23:47.200
Seeker: N- really? No halflings have a place that they're from?

00:23:49.020 --> 00:23:59.440
Wellby: They have a place they're from, like, for a generation or two, but, you know, families that are associated with a city or a town or a trade route, but everybody moves eventually.

00:23:59.740 --> 00:24:00.060
DM: Hmm.

00:24:00.080 --> 00:24:01.940
Wellby: Like, no one's there permanently.

00:24:02.420 --> 00:24:06.840
Kenzo: But aren't you all connected through, through One Song, and you have a deep sense of, you know-

00:24:08.900 --> 00:24:10.340
Wellby: Well, my great-grandfather would say-

00:24:10.340 --> 00:24:10.580
Unknown: ... community memory

00:24:10.580 --> 00:24:11.920
Wellby: ... we're all waves on the ocean.

00:24:13.060 --> 00:24:15.280
Wellby: He said a lot of weird shit that didn't make any sense.

00:24:15.580 --> 00:24:16.240
DM: Hmm.

00:24:16.260 --> 00:24:16.380
Unknown: Hmm.

00:24:17.496 --> 00:24:19.416
Wellby: I think there's something to that.

00:24:19.416 --> 00:24:20.836
Wellby: But I think he also meant that, like,

00:24:21.816 --> 00:24:25.516
Wellby: the settlements and the trade routes also were waves on the ocean, and that the,

00:24:26.376 --> 00:24:34.676
Wellby: the cities would change their names, you know, through centuries and millennia, but you still move through them in these rhythms and patterns.

00:24:35.036 --> 00:24:35.796
Seeker: Hmm.

00:24:35.796 --> 00:24:36.776
Wellby: Like, the movement

00:24:37.836 --> 00:24:44.596
Wellby: of our people across the land is itself, you know, a sort of a song with repeated choruses, even as the words change.

00:24:46.356 --> 00:24:47.516
Seeker: How do halflings retire?

00:24:49.775 --> 00:24:51.676
Wellby: Uh, don't really.

00:24:51.676 --> 00:24:51.896
Wellby: I mean,

00:24:53.656 --> 00:24:55.576
Wellby: there's mutual aid and care, but like a-

00:24:56.096 --> 00:24:56.156
Unknown: Hmm

00:24:56.156 --> 00:24:58.676
Wellby: ... the ships are all family ships.

00:24:58.676 --> 00:24:58.936
Wellby: So it's-

00:24:58.936 --> 00:24:59.836
Seeker: They just stay

00:24:59.836 --> 00:25:02.196
Wellby: ... you know, it's not that everyone is just workers there.

00:25:02.196 --> 00:25:03.476
Wellby: It's family, and there's...

00:25:03.476 --> 00:25:07.056
Wellby: I mean, there's me at age three is not getting a lot of work done on the ship.

00:25:07.576 --> 00:25:07.876
Wellby: Hmm.

00:25:07.896 --> 00:25:11.156
Wellby: My great-great-grandfather was not getting a lot of work done on the ship.

00:25:14.176 --> 00:25:14.336
Unknown: Hmm.

00:25:15.316 --> 00:25:17.516
Wellby: Hmm. But we don't leave people behind.

00:25:18.036 --> 00:25:18.336
Seeker: Yeah.

00:25:18.336 --> 00:25:18.656
Wellby: You know?

00:25:19.536 --> 00:25:20.976
Kenzo: Well, it seems almost sort of like, like-

00:25:21.076 --> 00:25:22.596
Unknown: I mean, we don't leave halflings behind.

00:25:22.656 --> 00:25:24.036
Kenzo: Yeah, yeah, of course.

00:25:24.336 --> 00:25:25.976
Kenzo: But it, but it also seems almost like,

00:25:27.116 --> 00:25:28.796
Kenzo: you know, nowhere is home, but everywhere is home.

00:25:32.236 --> 00:25:32.636
Wellby: Yeah.

00:25:34.096 --> 00:25:37.196
Seeker: Since we're about to cross the Western Gulf

00:25:38.296 --> 00:25:38.796
Seeker: in the air,

00:25:41.136 --> 00:25:42.416
Seeker: what was the, uh...

00:25:43.656 --> 00:25:44.756
Seeker: I'm trying to think of the question here.

00:25:45.856 --> 00:25:46.716
Wellby: Should we go for a swim?

00:25:47.176 --> 00:25:50.756
Seeker: What was the craziest thing that happened to you in your days on the gulf?

00:25:51.656 --> 00:25:52.736
Seeker: Is there anything we should look out for?

00:25:53.736 --> 00:25:57.996
Seeker: For example, if we were to dive the ship a little lower, that might jump up out of the gulf and bite us?

00:25:58.396 --> 00:25:58.456
Wellby: So-

00:25:58.456 --> 00:26:01.016
Seeker: Not that I'm constantly alert to that kind of possibility, but...

00:26:01.116 --> 00:26:06.596
Wellby: Here's the thing. When I was nine years old, I saw a dragon in the gulf.

00:26:06.796 --> 00:26:06.996
Unknown: No.

00:26:07.016 --> 00:26:10.656
Wellby: And it was underwater, and it was easily larger than this ship.

00:26:12.096 --> 00:26:13.176
Seeker: Larger than this ship?

00:26:13.796 --> 00:26:15.936
Wellby: None of the other people on my boat saw this.

00:26:16.216 --> 00:26:16.236
Unknown: Hmm.

00:26:16.236 --> 00:26:20.916
Wellby: And they gave me shit about it for years, but I am 100% positive.

00:26:21.716 --> 00:26:22.216
Wellby: It, I,

00:26:23.276 --> 00:26:25.495
Unknown: it is so big.

00:26:25.495 --> 00:26:27.536
Wellby: They're like, "Oh, no, it's a bunch of whales."

00:26:28.876 --> 00:26:29.156
Kenzo: Do you think it-

00:26:29.256 --> 00:26:33.476
Wellby: It's not like one interconnected whale in a line with, like, side whales coming out as legs.

00:26:33.536 --> 00:26:34.376
Seeker: Are there whales in the gulf?

00:26:34.956 --> 00:26:38.096
Wellby: Oh, yeah. Sure. All, all sorts, and, and whaling too.

00:26:38.476 --> 00:26:41.956
Kenzo: Do you think if we got closer with the ship to the water that we would kind of call it out?

00:26:43.936 --> 00:26:48.276
Wellby: I don't know. I mean, I only saw it that once, and I never met anybody who saw it.

00:26:48.576 --> 00:26:49.636
Seeker: What part of the gulf were you in?

00:26:49.636 --> 00:26:51.356
Wellby: And it's possible I was dreaming.

00:26:52.276 --> 00:26:52.896
Seeker: You were a kid.

00:26:53.516 --> 00:26:53.936
Wellby: Mm-hmm.

00:26:53.936 --> 00:26:58.556
Seeker: But sometimes kids are able to see things that adults can't because they're not expecting them.

00:26:58.636 --> 00:26:59.876
Wellby: I'm sure I saw it.

00:27:00.356 --> 00:27:07.776
Wellby: But in the gulf, the whaling is only the young whales, and the old ones are hundreds and hundreds and hundreds of years old.

00:27:07.776 --> 00:27:10.616
Wellby: And you can never hunt the elders-

00:27:10.656 --> 00:27:10.756
Unknown: Hmm

00:27:10.756 --> 00:27:11.236
Wellby: ... only the

00:27:12.596 --> 00:27:13.236
Wellby: young ones.

00:27:13.516 --> 00:27:14.896
Kenzo: Tim, I, uh, commune with nature.

00:27:17.556 --> 00:27:18.416
DM: Okay.

00:27:18.416 --> 00:27:20.776
Kenzo: Are there any, uh, like, fucking epic

00:27:21.896 --> 00:27:23.256
Kenzo: sea things in the, the Western Gulf?

00:27:23.276 --> 00:27:25.176
Seeker: Well, we're not quite over the gulf yet, or maybe we are.

00:27:25.196 --> 00:27:25.436
Unknown: We're two days out.

00:27:25.496 --> 00:27:27.576
Unknown: Yeah. You're, you're still on land right now [laughs].

00:27:27.596 --> 00:27:27.636
Unknown: Oh.

00:27:27.636 --> 00:27:28.736
Wellby: Doing nothing suspicious.

00:27:28.736 --> 00:27:31.936
Delwath: Tim's like, "Well, I prepared 12 hours in Tollen."

00:27:31.936 --> 00:27:32.316
Unknown: [laughs]

00:27:32.316 --> 00:27:36.976
Delwath: "But if they get to Tollen, then by the time the adventure's over, uh, I'll be surprised."

00:27:37.256 --> 00:27:41.196
Kenzo: Well, uh, just once we get to the Western Gulf, I'll, I'm gonna, I wanna see if there's any sea creatures.

00:27:41.976 --> 00:27:42.336
Wellby: Yeah.

00:27:42.356 --> 00:27:44.596
Kenzo: I'll commune with nature and see if there's any, uh...

00:27:44.656 --> 00:27:46.596
Seeker: Oh, and Tim, are there underwater dragons?

00:27:46.596 --> 00:27:48.756
Seeker: Is that a thing that I know about arcana-wise?

00:27:49.216 --> 00:27:49.476
DM: Um,

00:27:50.496 --> 00:27:51.456
DM: make an arcana check.

00:27:53.176 --> 00:27:53.216
Unknown: Yes.

00:27:53.236 --> 00:27:54.236
Wellby: I'll make an arcana check too.

00:27:56.236 --> 00:27:58.836
Seeker: That's 15 on the die, so I...

00:27:59.056 --> 00:27:59.596
Seeker: It's a 20.

00:27:59.676 --> 00:28:00.816
DM: You have plus, like, 27, so.

00:28:00.896 --> 00:28:02.056
Seeker: Yeah, it's a 20.

00:28:02.296 --> 00:28:02.336
DM: Plus 14.

00:28:02.416 --> 00:28:02.876
Seeker: It's a 20.

00:28:02.876 --> 00:28:03.176
Wellby: Holy shit.

00:28:04.076 --> 00:28:05.636
Unknown: What, plus 14 arcana?

00:28:05.656 --> 00:28:05.976
DM: Yeah, so, so-

00:28:05.996 --> 00:28:06.616
Seeker: Plus 13.

00:28:06.836 --> 00:28:07.076
DM: You, you-

00:28:07.096 --> 00:28:07.656
Kenzo: Goddamn

00:28:07.656 --> 00:28:08.896
DM: ... you've certainly...

00:28:08.896 --> 00:28:11.816
DM: I mean, what has Seeker read about underwater dragons?

00:28:12.156 --> 00:28:13.756
Kenzo: That they're everywhere.

00:28:13.756 --> 00:28:15.736
Unknown: [laughs]

00:28:15.736 --> 00:28:17.116
Wellby: They're friendly.

00:28:17.116 --> 00:28:19.296
Wellby: They especially love airships.

00:28:19.296 --> 00:28:21.676
Wellby: [laughs]

00:28:21.696 --> 00:28:24.196
Delwath: And there's one under us right now.

00:28:25.156 --> 00:28:28.196
Seeker: Underwater dragons are, um...

00:28:31.716 --> 00:28:37.755
Seeker: Man, I don't, I don't, I don't know if I know enough about our Talgar dragon lore generally to fit something into that, but...

00:28:39.496 --> 00:28:40.896
DM: Seeker, so you've read-

00:28:41.796 --> 00:28:42.656
Seeker: They're reclusive.

00:28:42.716 --> 00:28:43.756
Wellby: I think, I think they live in

00:28:45.336 --> 00:28:47.876
Wellby: air caves at the bottom of the ocean.

00:28:48.116 --> 00:28:49.356
Wellby: Like, you go down to the bottom-

00:28:49.596 --> 00:28:49.976
Seeker: Down

00:28:49.976 --> 00:28:50.336
Wellby: ... and up

00:28:51.136 --> 00:28:52.476
Wellby: into these caverns.

00:28:52.596 --> 00:28:52.616
Unknown: Hmm.

00:28:52.616 --> 00:28:53.856
Wellby: But they're still air breathers.

00:28:54.016 --> 00:28:54.136
Unknown: Oh.

00:28:54.136 --> 00:28:55.356
Seeker: So there's, like, air bubbles.

00:28:55.816 --> 00:28:57.476
Kenzo: Do... Wait, have comets ever hit Talgar?

00:28:58.396 --> 00:28:58.916
Seeker: Comets.

00:28:59.896 --> 00:29:00.816
DM: Yeah.

00:29:00.816 --> 00:29:07.276
Kenzo: 'Cause that's the... Then there must be, like, a network of cenotes, like, where the comets hit, and then there'd be, like, totally fucking air pockets down there.

00:29:07.316 --> 00:29:09.456
DM: Comets, so there was a comet that

00:29:10.516 --> 00:29:18.356
DM: that was a plot point in a one-shot I ran for Kayt and Isaac and Nathaniel once when we were doing the Rizwin adventures when she was separated from you.

00:29:19.116 --> 00:29:21.136
DM: You should ask Rizwin about comets sometime.

00:29:22.016 --> 00:29:22.536
Seeker: Interesting.

00:29:23.636 --> 00:29:28.476
Wellby: Maybe there are parts of the ocean where no one knows how deep the bottom is.

00:29:30.456 --> 00:29:30.496
Unknown: Hmm.

00:29:30.496 --> 00:29:35.576
Wellby: And it's not just, like, the eastern edge that goes off the map, but, like, there's a bottom.

00:29:35.636 --> 00:29:38.316
Seeker: Even in the center of the Western Gulf, there's some weird spots.

00:29:39.656 --> 00:29:42.476
Delwath: Well, and the Green Sea stretches quite a far-

00:29:42.556 --> 00:29:43.196
Seeker: It's large, yeah

00:29:43.196 --> 00:29:44.216
Delwath: ... way east.

00:29:44.236 --> 00:29:46.636
Unknown: Well, it's into the way on the eastern end too.

00:29:47.876 --> 00:29:49.076
Wellby: And in the loose sense.

00:29:50.796 --> 00:29:51.296
Kenzo: Well.

00:29:51.316 --> 00:29:51.556
Wellby: But yeah,

00:29:52.536 --> 00:29:53.456
Wellby: which is, like, 2 or 3,000 miles.

00:29:53.456 --> 00:29:54.696
Delwath: It just goes off the map here.

00:29:54.696 --> 00:29:54.696
DM: It's not on the map.

00:29:54.776 --> 00:29:54.896
Wellby: Yeah.

00:29:56.456 --> 00:29:59.496
Delwath: Oh, wow. But those islands out there are where all these white shops was.

00:29:59.516 --> 00:30:00.896
DM: Those islands are probably where we're probably heading.

00:30:00.916 --> 00:30:00.976
Wellby: Yep.

00:30:00.976 --> 00:30:02.516
Seeker: Oh, yeah. Oops, I dropped it.

00:30:02.556 --> 00:30:10.236
DM: So, but yes, there are, there are a variety of different underwater dragons, some which actually breathe water, and some which live in...

00:30:11.596 --> 00:30:21.720
DM: I'll use the plus minus at the top too to zoom in on the top left But the lore of

00:30:21.720 --> 00:30:24.800
DM: the mythology that Seeker has read of dragons is...

00:30:28.000 --> 00:30:32.240
DM: There is a lot of myth and it's rarely clear what is true.

00:30:32.680 --> 00:30:32.760
Unknown: Yeah.

00:30:32.760 --> 00:30:36.140
DM: Although one thing that seems to be quite often repeated is that-

00:30:36.380 --> 00:30:38.320
Unknown: Oh, I need something that isn't chocolate to snack on.

00:30:38.340 --> 00:30:40.000
Seeker: Mm-hmm. Sensible

00:30:40.000 --> 00:30:40.960
DM: ... is that, um,

00:30:43.360 --> 00:30:43.680
DM: so

00:30:45.860 --> 00:30:46.720
DM: there are

00:30:50.040 --> 00:30:55.540
DM: underwater... So the, like, oldest and eldest of the underwater dragons supposedly, um,

00:30:57.460 --> 00:30:58.120
DM: shape shift

00:30:59.680 --> 00:31:01.940
DM: and they'll take on forms of, like,

00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:06.080
DM: you know, the vi- ancient whales or the leviathans.

00:31:06.740 --> 00:31:06.880
Unknown: Cool.

00:31:08.540 --> 00:31:10.100
DM: And sometimes test people.

00:31:11.640 --> 00:31:11.820
Unknown: Hmm.

00:31:13.100 --> 00:31:13.440
Kenzo: That's awesome.

00:31:13.440 --> 00:31:19.260
Wellby: Maybe, maybe that's part of the mythology of why the ancient whales don't get hunted also, because sometimes they're dragons.

00:31:19.560 --> 00:31:22.100
Delwath: Well, Zir saw a leviathan who gave him a test.

00:31:22.700 --> 00:31:22.820
Unknown: Oh.

00:31:22.860 --> 00:31:24.060
Delwath: My Mawar character.

00:31:24.400 --> 00:31:25.020
Seeker: Amazing.

00:31:26.140 --> 00:31:29.360
Delwath: I'm like, "That's totally a dragon. We have to do a Mawar thing to go find the dragon now."

00:31:30.020 --> 00:31:30.700
Delwath: Sorry, keep going.

00:31:33.360 --> 00:31:39.240
DM: Um, but yes, according to halfling lore, part of the reason why you don't hunt elder whales is

00:31:41.260 --> 00:31:42.400
