01:12:03.602 --> 01:12:05.522
Delwath: We can all fly. If you can't find us-

01:12:05.842 --> 01:12:06.342
Kenzo: Fucking get back to the ship

01:12:06.342 --> 01:12:06.962
Delwath: ... fly up.

01:12:08.782 --> 01:12:08.902
DM: Um.

01:12:09.082 --> 01:12:10.422
Kenzo: The only one that can't fly is Seeker, right?

01:12:10.422 --> 01:12:11.182
Delwath: I am.

01:12:11.182 --> 01:12:12.062
DM: Seeker can't fly.

01:12:12.602 --> 01:12:14.662
Unknown: That's true. Oh. Oh, but you can-

01:12:14.782 --> 01:12:14.882
DM: Oh, my bad

01:12:14.882 --> 01:12:16.302
DM: ... if you animate something.

01:12:16.402 --> 01:12:18.442
Seeker: I, I can... Well, yeah.

01:12:18.482 --> 01:12:19.782
Delwath: I don't have the armor with me, but-

01:12:19.782 --> 01:12:21.002
Wellby: Oh, I'm so stoked.

01:12:21.482 --> 01:12:24.242
DM: Yeah, you do. It's in Wellby's, isn't it in Wellby's necklace?

01:12:24.282 --> 01:12:29.682
Kenzo: Oh. Tim, after, after Kenza gets his tattoos done, he's gonna go fucking, uh, go use his cloak of the manor and just fucking like go to the inn and order.

01:12:29.722 --> 01:12:29.942
DM: So,

01:12:31.062 --> 01:12:31.302
DM: um-

01:12:31.522 --> 01:12:32.382
Wellby: Yes

01:12:32.382 --> 01:12:32.682
DM: ... uh,

01:12:34.222 --> 01:12:35.762
DM: where? You're heading to the map maker first?

01:12:35.802 --> 01:12:36.542
Wellby: Map makers first.

01:12:37.022 --> 01:12:37.682
DM: Okay. So,

01:12:38.842 --> 01:12:49.242
DM: um, Wellby and Seeker head away from the sort of, um, busy, uh, sort of hectic part of

01:12:49.242 --> 01:12:51.602
DM: Tollen, the, away from the, um,

01:12:53.762 --> 01:12:54.602
DM: uh, the,

01:12:58.882 --> 01:13:01.442
DM: um, the, the sort of, um,

01:13:03.482 --> 01:13:13.522
DM: ancient, the oldest part of the city, the tight narrow streets, um, and head to, um, Scrollwright Street, which is this big broad street that runs

01:13:13.522 --> 01:13:21.002
DM: in between, um, the university area and, um, where there's a cluster of temples and sort of, um,

01:13:22.322 --> 01:13:27.162
DM: uh- And sort of big buildings and things like that.

01:13:27.162 --> 01:13:36.602
DM: And there's this broad street that's lined with booksellers and mapmakers and scroll makers and people selling minor magical charms and-

01:13:36.602 --> 01:13:37.302
Kenzo: And is this Midori?

01:13:38.502 --> 01:13:39.442
DM: Yeah, this is all Midori.

01:13:39.682 --> 01:13:40.542
Kenzo: It's so Midori.

01:13:40.542 --> 01:13:41.162
Wellby: It's pretty rad.

01:13:41.942 --> 01:13:44.542
DM: Um, and, and you walk along.

01:13:44.542 --> 01:13:48.822
DM: Wellby is like, it's probably been what, five years since you've been in Tollen?

01:13:48.982 --> 01:13:49.802
Wellby: It must be, yeah.

01:13:50.102 --> 01:13:50.902
DM: Something like that.

01:13:52.402 --> 01:13:56.762
DM: Um, the, the mapmaker's name is, um, Guy Marchand, and he's-

01:13:57.342 --> 01:13:58.242
Kenzo: Guy Marchand?

01:13:58.242 --> 01:13:59.242
DM: Yeah.

01:13:59.242 --> 01:13:59.582
DM: Nice.

01:14:00.042 --> 01:14:02.462
DM: He is in his 60s.

01:14:02.462 --> 01:14:06.182
DM: He's like... Well, he's probably in his 60s now, since you last saw him.

01:14:06.182 --> 01:14:09.302
DM: Um, maybe not quite in his 60s, maybe in his 50s.

01:14:09.302 --> 01:14:11.242
DM: Um, he's...

01:14:11.242 --> 01:14:16.402
DM: But you, he was, you know, this was the shop that, you know, you always would come into.

01:14:16.402 --> 01:14:20.982
DM: He sold you your like, uh, what was the name of your book for your backstory?

01:14:21.002 --> 01:14:22.062
DM: Like, whatever was your-

01:14:22.222 --> 01:14:22.862
Wellby: The first, yeah

01:14:22.862 --> 01:14:25.202
DM: ... first book on, on mapmaking.

01:14:25.202 --> 01:14:25.362
DM: Um,

01:14:27.202 --> 01:14:31.342
DM: and you walk along the street, like just kind of remembering,

01:14:32.522 --> 01:14:33.782
DM: remembering this path.

01:14:33.782 --> 01:14:39.202
DM: Like, it's almost exactly the journey you would make when your ship docked and you came on onto the like busy...

01:14:39.202 --> 01:14:41.682
DM: Your family would go off and, you know, sell

01:14:43.322 --> 01:14:50.762
DM: their goods and make connections with halflings and things like that, and you would go see what new maps Guy has for me to look at.

01:14:52.222 --> 01:14:59.282
DM: So you walk along the street and, and you see his shop and it's, it's probably like 3:00 in the afternoon at this point, 2:00 in the afternoon.

01:14:59.302 --> 01:14:59.522
DM: Um,

01:15:00.862 --> 01:15:03.842
DM: and you enter and he's, his shop is like chaotic.

01:15:03.842 --> 01:15:05.262
Wellby: This is so cool.

01:15:05.262 --> 01:15:05.482
DM: Um,

01:15:06.702 --> 01:15:08.462
DM: you know, he's...

01:15:08.462 --> 01:15:10.062
DM: There's like maps everywhere.

01:15:10.062 --> 01:15:11.542
DM: There's like papers draped.

01:15:11.542 --> 01:15:12.882
DM: There's like, um...

01:15:12.882 --> 01:15:19.682
DM: And he's sort of sitting, um, looking at this like big open folio wearing, again, the sort of Tollen red.

01:15:19.682 --> 01:15:21.762
DM: Um, you know, he's pretty well off, right?

01:15:21.762 --> 01:15:25.642
DM: Like he's, you know, um, he seems to do well for himself.

01:15:27.062 --> 01:15:34.061
DM: And he, he, as the bell rings as he open, as the door opens, he's, he just says, "Yes, can I help you?"

01:15:35.622 --> 01:15:36.542
DM: not looking up.

01:15:38.962 --> 01:15:43.062
Wellby: It's been a long time. You sold me my first book of maps, and I'm back with corrections.

01:15:44.542 --> 01:15:52.442
DM: A smi- as he hears you, this smile comes across his otherwise what sort of serious face, and he looks up and he says, "Wellby,

01:15:53.602 --> 01:15:56.982
DM: it's been quite some time, has it not?"

01:15:57.662 --> 01:15:59.482
Wellby: It's been a long time. You're looking well.

01:16:00.562 --> 01:16:01.642
Wellby: Shop's the same as ever.

01:16:02.642 --> 01:16:03.522
DM: You too, and

01:16:05.382 --> 01:16:06.262
DM: with a friend.

01:16:07.062 --> 01:16:07.482
Seeker: Seeker.

01:16:08.642 --> 01:16:09.542
Seeker: Pleased to meet you.

01:16:09.542 --> 01:16:10.602
DM: Seeker after what?

01:16:12.482 --> 01:16:13.862
Seeker: All manner of things.

01:16:15.642 --> 01:16:17.142
DM: Are you here to buy or sell?

01:16:18.642 --> 01:16:19.002
Wellby: Both.

01:16:20.382 --> 01:16:21.942
Unknown: But let me start by showing you what I've got.

01:16:21.942 --> 01:16:32.022
Wellby: And I'll reach into my sack. I'll pull out the, um, you know, the map book and start flipping through to find the corrections and annotations that I've made, and then also

01:16:32.022 --> 01:16:34.802
Wellby: start pulling out all of the maps of Eastern Dunmar,

01:16:36.502 --> 01:16:38.242
Wellby: and just spreading them out.

01:16:38.862 --> 01:16:48.282
DM: He's like clearing like junk, you know, pushing stuff aside as you, as you, um, as you spread things out, and he's like folding up this big folio that he's working on and he...

01:16:49.502 --> 01:16:49.942
DM: Wow.

01:16:52.002 --> 01:16:53.442
DM: Fascinating.

01:16:53.442 --> 01:16:55.622
DM: You have really traveled, have you not?

01:16:55.722 --> 01:16:58.562
Wellby: Covered a lot of ground in the last year.

01:16:59.502 --> 01:17:03.022
Wellby: A lot, a lot of ground. From here through Dunmar to Charn to

01:17:04.562 --> 01:17:05.122
Wellby: the east,

01:17:06.562 --> 01:17:09.042
Wellby: east of the whatever the name of the river is that I forget.

01:17:11.022 --> 01:17:11.722
DM: The Maha.

01:17:11.722 --> 01:17:12.062
Wellby: Mm.

01:17:12.062 --> 01:17:13.262
DM: Yeah, the... Right.

01:17:14.062 --> 01:17:15.562
Seeker: Not the safest place to travel.

01:17:17.142 --> 01:17:19.082
Wellby: No. It's been a little dicey sometimes.

01:17:20.942 --> 01:17:22.402
DM: What brings you back to Tollen?

01:17:24.622 --> 01:17:27.202
Wellby: Well, strange thing. We're looking for volcanoes.

01:17:27.202 --> 01:17:30.122
Unknown: [laughs]

01:17:30.122 --> 01:17:35.382
Wellby: And not just like, "Oh look, there's a volcano," but the biggest of the big.

01:17:35.942 --> 01:17:36.542
DM: Really hot.

01:17:38.342 --> 01:17:39.182
Kenzo: Elementally hot.

01:17:39.502 --> 01:17:42.642
DM: There's a lot of volcanoes up in the Skaer Islands.

01:17:44.601 --> 01:17:47.222
Unknown: Islands that have fire elementals, you know.

01:17:48.882 --> 01:17:50.842
Unknown: Yeah. I'm looking for...

01:17:51.742 --> 01:17:53.162
Wellby: I don't know. How would you describe it?

01:17:54.902 --> 01:17:55.322
Seeker: Magic.

01:17:57.262 --> 01:17:58.542
DM: Magic volcanoes.

01:17:58.862 --> 01:17:59.322
Kenzo: There you go.

01:17:59.662 --> 01:18:01.682
DM: Yeah, you should talk to somebody at the university then.

01:18:02.502 --> 01:18:02.622
Unknown: Hmm.

01:18:04.182 --> 01:18:06.002
Seeker: Do you, uh, do you have any connections there?

01:18:08.782 --> 01:18:11.822
DM: Yeah, I could introduce you to a student I know there.

01:18:11.822 --> 01:18:12.082
DM: Um,

01:18:14.422 --> 01:18:17.602
DM: after you left, Wellby, uh, the shop was kind of quiet.

01:18:18.842 --> 01:18:19.102
DM: Um,

01:18:21.322 --> 01:18:23.142
DM: there was this young woman from,

01:18:25.342 --> 01:18:26.782
DM: from down south. She,

01:18:27.922 --> 01:18:28.122
DM: hmm,

01:18:29.242 --> 01:18:30.442
DM: she was, uh...

01:18:31.982 --> 01:18:35.182
DM: My family was, was originally from Isingue.

01:18:35.182 --> 01:18:36.422
DM: You might not know.

01:18:36.422 --> 01:18:37.442
DM: I think I told you that.

01:18:37.462 --> 01:18:38.362
Seeker: Mm-hmm. Mm-hmm.

01:18:39.762 --> 01:18:42.302
DM: There's a few of us from there.

01:18:42.322 --> 01:18:42.542
DM: Um,

01:18:44.302 --> 01:18:47.562
DM: you know, of course, it was destroyed in the Great War,

01:18:48.682 --> 01:18:53.242
DM: covered by the plague. Used to be supposedly this grand place on the river.

01:18:54.242 --> 01:18:56.162
Wellby: Isingue, where the two rivers come together?

01:18:58.622 --> 01:18:58.942
DM: Yeah.

01:18:59.762 --> 01:19:00.302
Wellby: Yeah.

01:19:01.602 --> 01:19:03.142
Wellby: It's not in great shape these days.

01:19:04.082 --> 01:19:06.182
Unknown: Wait, you've been there?

01:19:06.522 --> 01:19:08.622
Wellby: Yeah, we flew over it a few days ago.

01:19:08.642 --> 01:19:09.822
Wellby: Well, we've got this, uh-

01:19:09.942 --> 01:19:10.662
DM: Wait, you flew?

01:19:10.682 --> 01:19:12.222
Unknown: Yeah, we got this flying, um-

01:19:12.702 --> 01:19:13.542
DM: It's a mirror

01:19:13.542 --> 01:19:15.522
Wellby: ... uh, ship from the giants from ages past.

01:19:15.522 --> 01:19:18.042
Wellby: [laughs]

01:19:18.042 --> 01:19:21.002
DM: [laughs] He's just like, "Gods,

01:19:22.162 --> 01:19:23.722
DM: you've changed."

01:19:24.942 --> 01:19:26.342
Wellby: Th- there's been a lot of miles.

01:19:26.742 --> 01:19:27.702
Wellby: A lot of miles.

01:19:28.782 --> 01:19:31.386
DM: Five years is- Me?

01:19:32.546 --> 01:19:33.666
DM: Yeah. Okay

01:19:33.666 --> 01:19:33.846
Wellby: Hmm

01:19:35.006 --> 01:19:37.246
DM: Anyway, I can introduce you to her.

01:19:37.586 --> 01:19:38.646
DM: Um, she comes by the shop

01:19:40.146 --> 01:19:41.186
DM: not infrequently.

01:19:42.066 --> 01:19:43.986
Unknown: That'd be wonderful. That'd be helpful.

01:19:43.986 --> 01:19:45.386
Seeker: You said she's a student at the university?

01:19:45.606 --> 01:19:45.886
DM: Yeah.

01:19:45.926 --> 01:19:47.066
Seeker: And what's she a student of?

01:19:50.166 --> 01:19:54.706
DM: Not sure she's quite sure what she wants to be a student of.

01:19:54.706 --> 01:19:55.046
DM: She...

01:19:58.466 --> 01:20:03.446
DM: Her father is a marcher lord way down south in the Duchy of Massao,

01:20:05.026 --> 01:20:05.406
DM: and

01:20:07.366 --> 01:20:10.706
DM: I guess he sent her north 'cause she had some minor talent for magic.

01:20:14.366 --> 01:20:15.786
DM: Learn to control it, I guess.

01:20:16.186 --> 01:20:16.406
Unknown: Hmm.

01:20:19.106 --> 01:20:20.306
Seeker: Sounds like somebody we should meet.

01:20:21.966 --> 01:20:22.886
DM: Yeah, happy to.

01:20:23.706 --> 01:20:25.646
DM: I'll send her a letter and arrange a time.

01:20:25.646 --> 01:20:26.746
DM: Where can I reach you at?

01:20:29.886 --> 01:20:31.206
Delwath: The giant flying ship in the s-

01:20:31.266 --> 01:20:35.405
Wellby: The flying ship docked in the harbor above the water.

01:20:35.405 --> 01:20:37.366
Unknown: [laughs]

01:20:37.386 --> 01:20:40.006
Wellby: Wellby says in just that tone of tenderness.

01:20:40.626 --> 01:20:43.546
DM: Oh, Wellby, what has happened to you?

01:20:44.026 --> 01:20:44.406
Wellby: Yeah.

01:20:45.366 --> 01:20:45.846
DM: Um,

01:20:50.426 --> 01:20:53.966
DM: I guess that won't be hard for a messenger to find.

01:20:54.966 --> 01:20:59.926
Wellby: Yeah, there's some, uh, of the harbor guard stationed on it, so I'm sure they can take a message also.

01:21:01.086 --> 01:21:01.786
DM: Okay.

01:21:01.866 --> 01:21:03.626
Wellby: Yeah. Uh.

01:21:03.726 --> 01:21:04.306
DM: Uh.

01:21:04.326 --> 01:21:05.806
Wellby: This is new for me also.

01:21:08.306 --> 01:21:08.406
DM: I...

01:21:09.946 --> 01:21:14.486
DM: When you walked in, I remembered I had set something aside for you I thought you'd be interested in.

01:21:14.546 --> 01:21:15.506
Wellby: Really?

01:21:15.506 --> 01:21:15.546
DM: But

01:21:15.566 --> 01:21:15.726
Wellby: Hmm

01:21:17.486 --> 01:21:18.446
DM: I don't know now.

01:21:19.226 --> 01:21:20.846
Wellby: I'm endlessly curious.

01:21:20.866 --> 01:21:21.266
Wellby: What is it?

01:21:22.886 --> 01:21:23.106
DM: Well,

01:21:24.526 --> 01:21:25.846
DM: I, after what you said, I mean...

01:21:28.526 --> 01:21:30.086
Unknown: Well, I, I, hmm.

01:21:30.126 --> 01:21:33.246
Wellby: I have to tell you about the islands at the eastern end of the Green Sea.

01:21:33.246 --> 01:21:33.926
Wellby: You're gonna love this.

01:21:34.806 --> 01:21:35.006
DM: Wait.

01:21:35.006 --> 01:21:35.926
Wellby: But let's come back to that.

01:21:36.246 --> 01:21:38.846
DM: Into the eastern end of the Green Sea?

01:21:38.846 --> 01:21:40.486
Wellby: The islands move.

01:21:41.326 --> 01:21:41.686
Seeker: Do they?

01:21:43.726 --> 01:21:44.646
DM: How are they doing that?

01:21:44.646 --> 01:21:46.186
Seeker: Not quickly, presumably.

01:21:46.186 --> 01:21:47.006
Wellby: Not all of them.

01:21:50.566 --> 01:21:52.806
DM: He gets up and he walks over and he, like,

01:21:53.966 --> 01:21:57.966
DM: pulls through his shelf and he, and he pulls out this, this scroll.

01:21:59.626 --> 01:22:03.926
DM: Um, he brings it over and he unrolls it, and it's a, um, it's a map.

01:22:04.966 --> 01:22:07.906
DM: Like a, a sort of a perspective map.

01:22:07.906 --> 01:22:12.226
DM: It's not like a, it's not like a nice detailed, like, um, clear map.

01:22:12.226 --> 01:22:15.306
DM: It's, it's almost like a drawing from a, from a perspective.

01:22:15.306 --> 01:22:15.426
DM: Um,

01:22:16.426 --> 01:22:21.846
DM: he says, "I found this recently, a map of the mountains before the Great War.

01:22:23.666 --> 01:22:24.846
DM: Thought you'd be curious."

01:22:24.846 --> 01:22:26.826
Unknown: The mountains before the Great War?

01:22:27.246 --> 01:22:28.906
DM: Yeah. You know how they're all like...

01:22:28.906 --> 01:22:29.966
DM: You know, there's all this like-

01:22:29.966 --> 01:22:30.746
Wellby: They got rearranged.

01:22:31.026 --> 01:22:32.166
DM: Yeah.

01:22:32.166 --> 01:22:33.706
Seeker: I'm especially interested in that.

01:22:33.706 --> 01:22:34.586
Seeker: One among my...

01:22:35.766 --> 01:22:37.686
Seeker: The areas in which I seek knowledge is-

01:22:37.786 --> 01:22:38.806
DM: Oh, you? Really?

01:22:38.806 --> 01:22:41.246
Seeker: ... the shapes and movements of the earth itself.

01:22:41.586 --> 01:22:42.806
DM: Wait, how old is Seeker?

01:22:44.186 --> 01:22:44.446
Seeker: Uh.

01:22:45.086 --> 01:22:46.406
DM: Like s- s- s-

01:22:46.406 --> 01:22:47.226
Seeker: Not, not super old

01:22:47.226 --> 01:22:47.246
DM: ... 16.

01:22:47.246 --> 01:22:47.806
Delwath: He's young.

01:22:47.826 --> 01:22:47.846
Wellby: Yeah.

01:22:47.846 --> 01:22:50.266
Delwath: So he wouldn't, he pre- he's, he wouldn't have remembered the Great War.

01:22:50.286 --> 01:22:50.746
DM: No.

01:22:50.746 --> 01:22:52.346
Seeker: No. But

01:22:53.886 --> 01:22:57.466
Seeker: the rising and falling of mountain ranges is definitely on his list of curiosities.

01:22:57.486 --> 01:22:58.966
Wellby: Wait, what changed about the mountains?

01:22:59.406 --> 01:23:01.126
Unknown: Like the giant section of them that like-

01:23:01.166 --> 01:23:02.046
Seeker: That got destroyed?

01:23:02.046 --> 01:23:02.086
Wellby: [gasps]

01:23:02.086 --> 01:23:02.686
DM: Yeah, okay.

01:23:02.726 --> 01:23:05.126
DM: And others of them like rose and others of them sunk.

01:23:05.126 --> 01:23:06.686
Seeker: Yep, yep, yep.

01:23:06.686 --> 01:23:07.926
Seeker: And so this is a map from what...

01:23:08.926 --> 01:23:09.486
Seeker: I, I, uh,

01:23:10.526 --> 01:23:14.706
Seeker: get myself around in front of it. What, what perspective does it look like we're viewing the world from?

01:23:16.786 --> 01:23:17.506
DM: It's Sembara.

01:23:18.026 --> 01:23:20.746
Seeker: Mm-hmm. And looking south or something?

01:23:21.006 --> 01:23:22.306
DM: Eh, looking west.

01:23:22.306 --> 01:23:22.986
Seeker: Looking west.

01:23:24.346 --> 01:23:24.826
Seeker: From, from a point-

01:23:24.866 --> 01:23:25.686
Wellby: But we've never been-

01:23:25.686 --> 01:23:27.066
Seeker: ... thousands of feet in the air, though.

01:23:27.146 --> 01:23:27.526
DM: Yeah

01:23:27.526 --> 01:23:28.926
Wellby: ... anywhere close to that, right?

01:23:29.006 --> 01:23:29.206
DM: No.

01:23:30.586 --> 01:23:32.206
Seeker: Who, where did you get this map from?

01:23:34.866 --> 01:23:36.666
Unknown: From a halfling trader, actually.

01:23:37.126 --> 01:23:37.126
Wellby: Oh.

01:23:39.086 --> 01:23:40.206
Wellby: Do you know how they came across it?

01:23:42.666 --> 01:23:43.126
Seeker: Is, is there-

01:23:43.246 --> 01:23:44.986
Unknown: I don't recall the story

01:23:44.986 --> 01:23:47.306
Seeker: ... is there any mark of a maker?

01:23:48.505 --> 01:23:50.706
DM: Um, it's not a complete map.

01:23:50.846 --> 01:23:51.586
DM: Ah.

01:23:51.586 --> 01:23:53.406
DM: Like, it's frayed around the edges.

01:23:54.606 --> 01:23:56.626
Seeker: Like it's literally a fragment of a larger thing?

01:23:58.866 --> 01:23:59.526
DM: Yeah.

01:23:59.546 --> 01:23:59.646
Unknown: Oh.

01:23:59.646 --> 01:24:01.186
Wellby: Who's the trader? Do you know that?

01:24:02.586 --> 01:24:04.126
Unknown: Um, it was, uh,

01:24:08.546 --> 01:24:10.846
DM: the Cottons.

01:24:10.846 --> 01:24:11.106
DM: They

01:24:12.306 --> 01:24:14.126
DM: usually go, uh,

01:24:15.566 --> 01:24:19.686
DM: south to Tallen and Embury from here over land,

01:24:21.226 --> 01:24:24.486
DM: but I only see them every other year or so.

01:24:24.486 --> 01:24:27.086
DM: I think they must be on a long route.

01:24:27.106 --> 01:24:28.426
Wellby: Hmm. All right.

01:24:29.946 --> 01:24:30.366
Wellby: Do we know-

01:24:30.646 --> 01:24:35.546
DM: Came by maybe six months ago from the north, they said.

01:24:37.286 --> 01:24:37.966
DM: Apparently,

01:24:40.406 --> 01:24:40.986
DM: there's

01:24:43.526 --> 01:24:45.826
DM: old things that one can find from,

01:24:46.926 --> 01:24:47.206
DM: uh,

01:24:48.526 --> 01:24:53.826
DM: back when Vasta was part of Greater Sembara in the years before the Great War.

01:24:53.926 --> 01:24:54.386
Wellby: Wow.

01:24:54.386 --> 01:24:56.926
Unknown: Some forts and castles up there apparently.

01:24:57.686 --> 01:24:58.926
Wellby: It's very close.

01:24:58.926 --> 01:24:59.746
DM: Yeah, I'm just thinking.

01:24:59.886 --> 01:25:00.066
Wellby: Yeah. Yeah.

01:25:00.086 --> 01:25:02.246
Unknown: That sounds, that sounds kind of cool.

01:25:02.606 --> 01:25:04.566
Unknown: Mm-hmm. This sounds awesome.

01:25:05.806 --> 01:25:06.446
Wellby: Fascinating.

01:25:07.286 --> 01:25:11.066
Kenzo: Sounds like our thing. Sounds like a, just the kind of side quest that could become a main quest.

01:25:11.586 --> 01:25:12.886
Seeker: As the maker of a map,

01:25:15.026 --> 01:25:15.106
Unknown: uh,

01:25:16.886 --> 01:25:18.906
Seeker: who would one be to be making this one?

01:25:19.866 --> 01:25:20.346
Wellby: Hmm.

01:25:20.346 --> 01:25:22.326
Seeker: You as maker of maps, you don't draw-

01:25:22.326 --> 01:25:22.486
DM: I was a geographer

01:25:22.486 --> 01:25:23.846
Seeker: ... aerial perspective views.

01:25:23.846 --> 01:25:24.126
Seeker: You do?

01:25:25.266 --> 01:25:25.426
DM: Yes. Look.

01:25:25.426 --> 01:25:26.306
Seeker: What makes you think that?

01:25:26.346 --> 01:25:31.466
DM: Points over. If you look, the heights of each mountain is very precise.

01:25:31.686 --> 01:25:34.646
Seeker: Hmm. That is indeed something that we care about.

01:25:35.626 --> 01:25:40.010
DM: I bet you there was a key once or some- ...

01:25:40.090 --> 01:25:47.570
DM: arrangement, and this is an elevation map that shows how tall above the ground each mountain goes.

01:25:47.570 --> 01:25:51.370
DM: Don't know who would be able to measure that other than dwarves or maybe Stoneborn.

01:25:52.010 --> 01:25:52.590
Seeker: Indeed.

01:25:54.350 --> 01:25:58.590
Seeker: And for what purpose would one make s- a map of such a large area

01:26:00.270 --> 01:26:01.710
Seeker: with the heights of mountains?

01:26:02.530 --> 01:26:03.890
DM: Oh, it's not that much an area.

01:26:04.430 --> 01:26:05.550
DM: It only shows, like,

01:26:06.950 --> 01:26:07.790
DM: two dozen mountains.

01:26:07.810 --> 01:26:07.950
Unknown: Hm.

01:26:07.950 --> 01:26:10.270
DM: It presumably once extended much further.

01:26:10.370 --> 01:26:10.830
Seeker: I see.

01:26:11.150 --> 01:26:12.610
Wellby: So this is like a fragment of a larger map.

01:26:12.610 --> 01:26:13.790
DM: It's a fragment of a larger map.

01:26:13.870 --> 01:26:15.630
Seeker: I'd, I'd love to see the rest of this.

01:26:18.530 --> 01:26:19.570
Wellby: You too, I suppose.

01:26:21.070 --> 01:26:21.950
DM: I'm curious.

01:26:22.930 --> 01:26:23.110
DM: Uh,

01:26:24.530 --> 01:26:25.630
DM: anyway, I thought you might want it, so there you go.

01:26:25.630 --> 01:26:26.890
Wellby: This is a fascinating find.

01:26:26.890 --> 01:26:27.810
Wellby: Thanks for thinking of me.

01:26:28.130 --> 01:26:29.170
Kenzo: It's a fascinating what?

01:26:29.170 --> 01:26:29.590
Wellby: Find.

01:26:30.370 --> 01:26:30.790
DM: So,

01:26:33.510 --> 01:26:34.450
DM: for friends?

01:26:35.550 --> 01:26:36.110
DM: Too bold?

01:26:39.150 --> 01:26:40.950
Wellby: Fair enough. But I'll do you one better.

01:26:40.950 --> 01:26:45.810
Wellby: Why don't I leave you my maps of, um, Eastern Dunmar?

01:26:47.790 --> 01:26:52.730
Wellby: Copy them over, and when we come back and find your student friend, I'll take them back again.

01:26:53.090 --> 01:26:57.850
DM: Eastern Dunmar is a bit of curiosity, but there's some people who might be

01:26:58.790 --> 01:27:04.670
DM: interested. It's not much practical use in the maps of Eastern Dunmar these days.

01:27:05.550 --> 01:27:06.690
Kenzo: Oh, you might find them useful later.

01:27:07.310 --> 01:27:11.190
Seeker: In fact, if you do meet someone especially interested in that area, we might like to know about it.

01:27:11.250 --> 01:27:12.870
Kenzo: Yeah [laughs]. Know who they are.

01:27:12.930 --> 01:27:14.990
Wellby: Yeah. But there may be in the years to come.

01:27:14.990 --> 01:27:17.750
Wellby: Dunmar is in a lot of motion right now.

01:27:17.750 --> 01:27:18.150
Delwath: Indeed.

01:27:19.470 --> 01:27:22.670
DM: Haven't had news from that far south in quite some time.

01:27:23.950 --> 01:27:29.410
Wellby: There is a awfully close threat of civil war between the current...

01:27:30.350 --> 01:27:33.010
Wellby: I have trouble with human politics, I'm sorry.

01:27:33.010 --> 01:27:39.570
Wellby: Between the current, uh, ruler and then his sister who was trapped in a magical prison for many years-

01:27:39.870 --> 01:27:40.630
DM: Did you see this movie?

01:27:40.630 --> 01:27:42.130
Unknown: ... that has come back again.

01:27:42.130 --> 01:27:42.410
Wellby: And-

01:27:42.510 --> 01:27:43.850
DM: Oh, did you already see this movie?

01:27:43.850 --> 01:27:46.070
Wellby: So, you know, it's happening.

01:27:46.070 --> 01:27:47.270
Seeker: Wellby's love to tell stories, you know.

01:27:48.110 --> 01:27:50.309
Delwath: Yeah, he's, like, terrible at secrets.

01:27:50.309 --> 01:27:51.850
Delwath: We know Wellby's terrible at secrets.

01:27:52.270 --> 01:27:53.930
DM: Well, certainly will be useful in my

01:27:55.070 --> 01:27:57.810
DM: map project. And he rolls out a map of, like,

01:27:59.390 --> 01:27:59.910
DM: half of

01:28:00.990 --> 01:28:01.710
DM: Taelgar.

01:28:01.790 --> 01:28:02.610
Seeker: Whoa.

01:28:03.290 --> 01:28:04.670
Wellby: Ugh, it's so beautiful.

01:28:05.670 --> 01:28:06.770
DM: I think yours might be better

01:28:07.610 --> 01:28:07.910
DM: by now.

01:28:07.910 --> 01:28:08.630
Wellby: Oh, it's coming.

01:28:09.490 --> 01:28:12.690
DM: When you started, you were quite envious of this, if I recall.

01:28:15.830 --> 01:28:17.930
Unknown: [coughs] I've been to a fair bit of it by now.

01:28:18.810 --> 01:28:18.950
Wellby: Mm.

01:28:20.330 --> 01:28:20.610
Wellby: All right.

01:28:20.610 --> 01:28:25.390
DM: Yeah, I think this is the inspiration for your first map even.

01:28:25.390 --> 01:28:25.550
Wellby: [coughs]

01:28:25.570 --> 01:28:25.910
DM: I think...

01:28:27.270 --> 01:28:31.830
DM: I don't know. You, I don't know when you, how, how long you knew him, uh, Seeker.

01:28:33.750 --> 01:28:34.050
Seeker: This one?

01:28:34.070 --> 01:28:36.230
DM: When he came in, the first time I met him,

01:28:37.650 --> 01:28:40.890
DM: he walked into my shop, the geomy place.

01:28:41.750 --> 01:28:42.650
Seeker: That sounds like Wellby.

01:28:43.990 --> 01:28:49.610
DM: And the first thing he said, I was actually working on this map when I said, "No, I'm closed."

01:28:51.050 --> 01:28:52.130
DM: I thought he'd just leave.

01:28:53.330 --> 01:28:58.470
DM: He just looked over my shoulder and he said, pointed to the farthest place on the map.

01:28:58.470 --> 01:28:59.070
Wellby: [laughs]

01:28:59.090 --> 01:29:01.430
DM: And he said, "I wonder what's there."

01:29:01.790 --> 01:29:03.570
Seeker: That also sounds just like Wellby.

01:29:04.150 --> 01:29:05.290
Kenzo: What was the point that he pointed at?

01:29:06.750 --> 01:29:08.510
DM: The farthest point on my map then

01:29:09.670 --> 01:29:11.730
Unknown: was way to the north,

01:29:13.670 --> 01:29:16.970
DM: beyond even where the Charbonian Empire stretches.

01:29:19.210 --> 01:29:20.330
DM: There's an island

01:29:22.070 --> 01:29:24.170
DM: that sits in between a peninsula,

01:29:25.350 --> 01:29:31.490
DM: it's called the Mawakel Peninsula, and the northern coast, which I have never mapped.

01:29:33.610 --> 01:29:35.590
DM: Big island out in the middle of the ocean.

01:29:37.790 --> 01:29:38.650
DM: Have you made it there yet?

01:29:40.290 --> 01:29:41.550
Wellby: Not yet.

01:29:41.550 --> 01:29:43.450
